## Root cause

`(*DefaultBinder).bindData` in `/echo/bind.go` had a single, type-blind map branch:

```go
// Map
if typ.Kind() == reflect.Map {
    for k, v := range data {
        val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(k), reflect.ValueOf(v[0]))
    }
    return nil
}
```

It always inserted the **first string** of each `data[key]` slice (`v[0]`) into the destination map, ignoring the map's element type. Consequences:
- `map[string]string` worked by luck (string → string).
- `map[string][]string` **panicked**: `reflect.Value.SetMapIndex: value of type string is not assignable to type []string` (bind.go:130 in the stack trace).
- Any other element type (`map[string]int`, non-string keys, …) also panicked instead of being ignored.
- Even when it didn't panic, all values after the first per key were silently dropped.

## Fix (only file changed: /echo/bind.go, lines 127–150; +20/-3)

Replaced the branch with type-aware, limited map support (same shape as upstream echo):

```go
	// Support binding to limited Map destinations:
	// - map[string][]string,
	// - map[string]string <-- (binds first value from data slice)
	// - map[string]interface{} <-- (binds slice of values)
	if typ.Kind() == reflect.Map && typ.Key().Kind() == reflect.String {
		k := typ.Elem().Kind()
		isElemInterface := k == reflect.Interface
		isElemString := k == reflect.String
		isElemSliceOfStrings := k == reflect.Slice && typ.Elem().Elem().Kind() == reflect.String

		if !(isElemSliceOfStrings || isElemString || isElemInterface) {
			return nil
		}
		for k, v := range data {
			if isElemString {
				if len(v) > 0 {
					val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(k), reflect.ValueOf(v[0]))
				}
			} else {
				val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(k), reflect.ValueOf(v))
			}
		}
		return nil
	}
```

Behaviour now: `map[string]string` → first value; `map[string][]string` → the whole value slice (all values, no dupes/losses); `map[string]interface{}` → the `[]string` slice; unsupported map element types (e.g. `map[string]int`

